"Large prime factors of well-distributed sequences (https://arxiv.org/html/2402.11884v4)\nciteturn9view0 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/html; Source: find({\"ref_id\":\"turn8view1\",\"pattern\":\"Theorem 7\"}); Total lines: 530\nL187: We will prove results comparing the distribution of $p_{1},p_{2},...$ to a Poisson-Dirichlet process (Theorem cite48†7 and Lemma cite49†8 ) and also an upper bound for the likelihood that $p_{1}=P^{+}(u)$ is exceptionally large (Theorem cite50†11 ). These results are related in that they use almost the same information, but because they may be of independent interest we have written this note so that their proofs may be read independently.\nL188: ###### Theorem 7.\nL189: \nL190: If $(a_{n})$ is $1$ well-distributed, then\nL191: \nL192:  | $$\\mathbb{P}\\Big(\\frac{\\log p_{1}}{\\log u}\\leq c_{1},...,\\frac{\\log p_{k}}{\\log u}\\leq c_{k}\\Big)\\rightarrow\\mathbb{P}(L_{1}\\leq c_{1},...,L_{k}\\leq c_{k}),$$  |\nL193: \nL194: as $x\\rightarrow\\infty$. That is, the process $\\tfrac{\\log p_{1}}{\\log u},\\tfrac{\\log p_{2}}{\\log u},...$ tends in distribution to the Poisson-Dirichlet process $L_{1},L_{2},...$.\nL195: This generalizes to multiple prime factors a result noted by Granville [cite51†22 ] (with details of the proof provided by Wang [cite52†44 ]) that for shifted primes the Elliott-Halberstam conjecture implies the distribution of the largest prime divisor is governed by the Dickman function (a phenomenon first conjectured by Pomerance [cite53†39 ]). And this result proves unconditionally that large prime factors of the Thue-Morse tend to a Poisson-Dirichlet distribution.\nL196: On the other hand, while the values of irreducible polynomials do not appear to have level of distribution $1$, it is reasonable to believe that their prime factors tend to a Poisson-Dirichlet distribution. That the distribution of the largest prime factor is governed by the Dickman function was given a conditional proof by Martin [cite54†32 ] on the assumption of a prime number theorem for polynomial sequences.\nL391: ###### Theorem 15 (An explicit upper bound sieve).\nL392: \nL393: For $\\mathcal{P}$, $P$, $g(d)$, and $r_{d}$ as just described, with $g$ satisfying (cite81†20 ), define $k=\\kappa+\\log K$. If a parameter $D$ is chosen such that all $p\\in\\mathcal{P}$ satisfy $p<D^{1/(4k)}$, then\nL394: \nL395:  | $$\\sum_{\\begin{subarray}{c}n\\leq x\\\\\nL396: (n,P)=1\\end{subarray}}a_{n}\\leq C\\cdot V\\cdot N(x)+\\sum_{\\begin{subarray}{c}d|P\\\\\nL397: d<D\\end{subarray}}\\tau_{3}(d)|r_{d}|,$$  |\nL398: \nL399: where $C>0$ is a constant which depends only on $k$, we have defined\nL400:  | $$V=\\prod_{p\\in\\mathcal{P}}(1-g(p)),$$  |\nL401: \nL402: and $\\tau_{3}(d)=\\sum_{d_{1}d_{2}d_{3}=d}1$ is the threefold divisor function.\nL403: ###### Proof.\nL404: \nL405: This is Theorem 7.4 in [cite79†19 ], where in their notation we have taken $s=4k$ and $X=N(x)$. (Note that the hypothesis of their theorem requires $g(d)\\in(0,1)$ for $d|P$, but one may check the proof works with no modification if $g(d)\\in[0,1)$ for $d|P$.) ∎\nL406: We now apply this result to get an upper bound on the frequency with which a number $n$ has a prime factor larger than $n^{1-\\epsilon}$. It is only when $\\epsilon$ is small that Theorem cite50†11 is nontrivial so we may suppose with no loss of generality that $\\epsilon<1/2$. The idea behind the proof is easy to state: if $n$ has a prime factor larger than $n^{1-\\epsilon}$, it will have no prime factors in between $n^{\\epsilon}$ and $n^{1-\\epsilon}$.\nL407: (Since $\\epsilon<1/2$, we have $n^{\\epsilon}<n^{1-\\epsilon}$.) The upper bound is obtained by sieving by (a subset of) such primes.\nL408: ###### Proof of Theorem cite50†11 .\nL409: \nL410: Note that by (cite37†3 ) there is a constant $z_{0}$ such that $g(p)\\leq 1/2$ for all $p\\geq z_{0}$. Further by (cite37†3 ) we have,\nL411: \nL412:  | $$\\prod_{z_{0}\\leq p<z}(1-g(p))=\\exp[-\\log\\log z+O(1)],$$  |\nL413: \nL414: so that under the hypothesis of Theorem cite50†11 , we have that (cite81†20 ) is satisfied for any subset $\\mathcal{P}$ of primes larger than $z_{0}$, for $\\kappa=1$ and some constant $K$. As in Theorem cite82†15 define $k=\\kappa+\\log K$.\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nLarge prime factors of well-distributed sequences (https://arxiv.org/html/2402.11884v4)\nciteturn9view1 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/html; Source: find({\"ref_id\":\"turn8view1\",\"pattern\":\"Theorem 11\"}); Total lines: 530\nL229: Lemma cite49†8 gives information about prime divisors of intermediate size, but because of restrictions on the support of $\\eta$ it does not entail an asymptotic formula for the distribution of the largest prime factor of $u$. Our last result shows that even this partial information about the level of distribution entails an upper bound for how often the largest prime factor can be especially large.\nL230: ###### Theorem 11.\nL231: \nL232: If $(a_{n})$ is $\\sigma$ well-distributed for some $\\sigma>0$, then for any $\\epsilon>0$,\nL233: \nL234:  | $$\\limsup_{x\\rightarrow\\infty}\\mathbb{P}(P^{+}(u)\\geq u^{1-\\epsilon})\\ll\\epsilon,$$  |\nL235: \nL236: where the implicit constant depends on the sequence $(a_{n})$.\nL237: We note that this is an essentially optimal result, since for sequences with level of distribution $1$ by Theorem cite48†7 we have $\\mathbb{P}(P^{+}(u)\\geq u^{1-\\epsilon})\\sim 1-\\rho(1/(1-\\epsilon))$, and for $\\epsilon$ small enough that $1\\leq 1/(1-\\epsilon)\\leq 2$ we have $1-\\rho(1/(1-\\epsilon))=\\log(1/(1-\\epsilon))\\approx\\epsilon$ (see [cite16†34 , (7.10)] for the evaluation of $\\rho$ in this range).\nL238: \nL239: In the case of sampling shifted primes $p-1\\leq x$, Theorem cite50†11 recovers the following corollary,\nL240: ###### Corollary 12 (Erdős).\nL241: \nL242: For any $\\epsilon>0$,\nL243: \nL244:  | $$\\limsup_{x\\rightarrow\\infty}\\frac{1}{\\pi(x)}|\\{p\\leq x:P^{+}(p-1)\\geq p^{1-\\epsilon}\\}|\\ll\\epsilon.$$  |\nL245: \nL246: This result appears implicitly, though somewhat obscurely, in a paper of Erdős (see the line beginning with “the sum in $a$ is less than” in [cite60†17 , p. 213]). A recent paper of Ding [cite61†15 ] gives a proof with explicit constants, and explains some of the history around this estimate.\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nSimple Proof of the Primitive Root Conjecture (https://arxiv.org/html/1707.06517)\nciteturn9view2 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/html; Source: find({\"ref_id\":\"turn7view0\",\"pattern\":\"Lemma 6.1\"}); Total lines: 540\nL15:   1. cite5†Abstract L16:   2. cite6†1 Introduction L17:   3. cite7†2 Characteristic Functions in Finite Fields L18:     1. cite8†2.1 Divisor Dependent Characteristic Function L19:     2. cite9†2.2 Divisorfree Characteristic Functions L20:   4. cite10†3 Finite Summation Kernel and Gauss Sum L21:     1. cite11†3.1 Finite Summation Kernel L22:     2. cite12†3.2 Gauss Sum L23:   5. cite13†4 Estimates of Power Exponential Sums L24:     1. cite14†4.1 Finite Fourier Transform of the Power Exponential Sum L25:   6. cite15†5 Evaluation of the Main Term L42: ###### Contents\nL43: \nL44:   1. cite6†1 Introduction L45:   2. cite7†2 Characteristic Functions in Finite Fields L46:   3. cite10†3 Finite Summation Kernel and Gauss Sum L47:   4. cite13†4 Estimates of Power Exponential Sums L48:   5. cite15†5 Evaluation of the Main Term L49:   6. cite16†6 Estimate for the Error Term L50:   7. cite17†7 Proof of Main Result L51:   8. cite18†8 Application To Repeated Decimals L52:   9. cite20†References L53: ## 1 Introduction\nL357: ## 6 Estimate for the Error Term\nL358: \nL359: The upper bounds for exponential sums over subsets of elements in finite fields $\\mathbb{F}_{p}$ studied in cite90†Section cite13†4 are used to estimate the error term $E(x)$ in the proof of cite47†Theorem cite48†1.1 .\nL360: ###### Lemma 6.1.\nL361: \nL362: Let $p\\geq 2$ be a large prime, let $\\psi\\neq 1$ be an additive character, and let $\\tau$ be a primitive root mod $p$. Assume the element $u\\neq 0$ is not a primitive root. Then,\nL363: \nL364:  | $$\\sum_{x\\leq p\\leq 2x}\\frac{1}{p}\\sum_{\\begin{subarray}{c}1\\leq n\\leq p-1\\\\\nL365: \\gcd(n,p-1)=1\\end{subarray}}\\sum_{0<k\\leq p-1}\\psi\\left((\\tau^{n}-u)k\\right)\\ll x^{1/2+\\delta}\\log x$$  |  | (6.1)\nL366: \nL367: for all sufficiently large numbers $x\\geq 1$ and an arbitrarily small number $\\delta>0$.\nL368: ###### Proof.\nL369: \nL370: Let $\\psi(z)=e^{i2\\pi z/p}$. By hypothesis $u\\neq\\tau^{n}$ for any $n\\geq 1$ such that $\\gcd(n,p-1)=1$. Rearrange the triple finite sum in the form of a discrete Fourier transform\nL371:  | $\\displaystyle E(x)$  | $\\displaystyle=$  | $\\displaystyle\\sum_{x\\leq p\\leq 2x}\\frac{1}{p}\\sum_{0<k\\leq p-1,}\\sum_{\\begin{subarray}{c}1\\leq n\\leq p-1\\\\\nL372: \\gcd(n,p-1)=1\\end{subarray}}\\psi((\\tau^{n}-u)k)$  |  | (6.2)\nL373:  |  | $\\displaystyle=$  | $\\displaystyle\\sum_{x\\leq p\\leq 2x}\\frac{1}{p}\\left(\\sum_{0<k\\leq p-1}e^{\\frac{-i2\\pi uk}{p}}\\sum_{\\begin{subarray}{c}1\\leq n\\leq p-1\\\\\nL374: \\gcd(n,p-1)=1\\end{subarray}}e^{\\frac{i2\\pi k\\tau^{n}}{p}}\\right).$  |\nL375: Applying cite91†Lemma cite92†4.1 to the inner double sum and completing the upper bound of the finite sum return\nL376: \nL377:  | $\\displaystyle|E(x)|$  | $\\displaystyle\\ll$  | $\\displaystyle\\sum_{x\\leq p\\leq 2x}\\frac{1}{p}\\cdot p^{1/2+\\delta}(\\log p)^{2}$  |  | (6.3)\nL378:  |  | $\\displaystyle\\ll$  | $\\displaystyle\\frac{(\\log x)^{2}}{x^{1/2-\\delta}}\\sum_{x\\leq p\\leq 2x}1$  |\nL379:  |  | $\\displaystyle\\ll$  | $\\displaystyle x^{1/2+\\delta}\\log x,$  |\nL388: ###### Proof.\nL389: \nL390: (cite47†Theorem cite48†1.1 ): Suppose that $u\\neq\\pm 1,v^{2}$ is not a primitive root for all primes $p\\geq x_{0}$, with $x_{0}\\geq 1$ constant. Let $x>x_{0}$ be a large number, and consider the sum of the characteristic function over the short interval $[x,2x]$, that is,\nL391: \nL392:  | $$\\pi_{u}(x)=\\sum_{x\\leq p\\leq 2x}\\Psi(u)=0.$$  |  | (7.2)\nL393: \nL394: Replacing the characteristic function, cite93†Lemma cite94†2.2 , and expanding the nonexistence equation (cite95†7.2 ) yield\nL395:  | $\\displaystyle\\pi_{u}(x)$  | $\\displaystyle=$  | $\\displaystyle\\sum_{x\\leq p\\leq 2x}\\Psi(u)$  |  | (7.3)\nL396:  |  | $\\displaystyle=$  | $\\displaystyle\\sum_{x\\leq p\\leq 2x}\\frac{1}{p}\\sum_{\\gcd(n,p-1)=1,}\\sum_{0\\leq k\\leq p-1}\\psi\\left((\\tau^{n}-u)k\\right)$  |\nL397:  |  | $\\displaystyle=$  | $\\displaystyle\\delta(u)\\sum_{x\\leq p\\leq 2x}\\frac{1}{p}\\sum_{\\gcd(n,p-1)=1}1+\\sum_{x\\leq p\\leq 2x}\\frac{1}{p}\\sum_{\\gcd(n,p-1)=1,}\\sum_{0<k\\leq p-1}\\psi\\left((\\tau^{n}-u)k\\right)$  |\nL399: where $\\delta(u)\\geq 0$ is a constant depending on the fixed integer $u\\neq 0$.\nL400: \nL401: The main term $M(x)$ is determined by a finite sum over the trivial additive character $\\psi=1$, and the error term $E(x)$ is determined by a finite sum over the nontrivial additive characters $\\psi(t)=e^{i2\\pi t/p}\\neq 1$.\nL402: \nL403: Applying cite96†Lemma cite97†5.2 to the main term, and cite98†Lemma cite99†6.1 to the error term yield\nL404:  | $\\displaystyle\\sum_{x\\leq p\\leq 2x}\\Psi(u)$  | $\\displaystyle=$  | $\\displaystyle\\delta(u)M(x)+E(x)$  |  | (7.4)\nL405:  |  | $\\displaystyle=$  | $\\displaystyle\\delta(u)\\left(\\text{li}(2x)-\\text{li}(x)\\right)+O\\left(\\frac{x}{(\\log x)^{B}}\\right)+O\\left(x^{1/2+\\delta}\\log x\\right)$  |\nL406:  |  | $\\displaystyle=$  | $\\displaystyle\\delta(u)\\frac{x}{\\log x}+O\\left(\\frac{x}{(\\log x)^{B}}\\right)$  |\nL407:  |  | $\\displaystyle>$  | $\\displaystyle 0,$  |\n"
